# Command mise

## What does this command do:

mise is a developer environment manager. When you enter a repo, mise ensures the right tool versions and environment variables are active. It is basically a cross between asdf, direnv, and a lightweight task runner.

## Installed from

For Fedora install using: curl https://mise.run | sh
For Raspberry Pi install using: curl https://mise.run | sh
For Mac install using: brew install mise

## Command path

For Fedora run: ~/.local/bin/mise
For Raspberry Pi run: ~/.local/bin/mise
For Mac run: /opt/homebrew/bin/mise

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at https://mise.jdx.dev/cli/

## Shell activation (add to ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc)

eval "$(mise activate bash)"   # bash
eval "$(mise activate zsh)"    # zsh

## Command options

# Install all tools defined in mise.toml / .tool-versions:
mise install

# Install a specific tool version:
mise install node@20
mise install node@20.0.0

# Set a tool version for the current project (writes mise.toml):
mise use node@20

# Set a global default tool version:
mise use -g node@20

# List installed and active tool versions:
mise ls

# List available remote versions for a tool:
mise ls-remote node

# Show outdated tools:
mise outdated

# Upgrade all tools (interactive):
mise up --interactive

# Upgrade a specific tool:
mise upgrade node

# Uninstall a tool version:
mise uninstall node@20

# Show current environment variables mise would set:
mise env

# Set an environment variable in mise.toml:
mise set NODE_ENV=production

# Unset an environment variable:
mise unset NODE_ENV

# Execute a command with mise environment active (one-off):
mise exec -- npm install
mise x node@20 -- node app.js

# Run a task defined in mise.toml:
mise run build
mise r build

# Watch and re-run a task on file changes:
mise watch build

# Show where a tool binary lives:
mise where node
mise which node

# Trust a mise.toml config file (required before mise loads it):
mise trust

# Check mise installation for problems:
mise doctor

# Activate mise in current shell (one-off, no rc edit needed):
mise activate bash

# Show mise settings:
mise settings

# Update mise itself:
mise self-update

# Remove mise and all managed data:
mise implode

# Generate shell completions:
mise completion bash
mise completion zsh
